# Evidence for a summary: Mia's summary of a paragraph of about 150 words leaves out one main point,
# and the child finds the line where that point starts. The answers are the line breaks at width 34
# (65 letters to a line), so the texts and the width stay as they are.
item reading.summary-missing v=1 skills=[reading.summary, reading.evidence] {
  title "What did the summary leave out?"
  difficulty pick(k, 0, 1, 1, 2)
  let k=0..3
  level easy {
    let k={0, 1}
  }
  level hard {
    let k={2, 3}
  }

  scene 35x40 {
    passage p title="{pick(k, \"The face we see\", \"Moon days\", \"Jumping on the moon\", \"Footprints\")}" text="{pick(k, \"The moon turns once on its axis in the time it takes to go once round the Earth, about twenty-seven days. Because of this, the same side of the moon always faces us, and the dark patches that people call the man in the moon never move. Nobody saw the far side until 1959, when a spacecraft called Luna 3 flew behind the moon and photographed it. It sent the pictures home by radio, and newspapers around the world printed them. They were blurred, but they held a surprise: the far side has hardly any dark patches and is covered in craters instead. People sometimes call it the dark side of the moon, but that is a mistake. The far side gets just as much sunlight as the side we see, and when our side has its long night, the far side has its long day. We are simply never in the right place to see it lit.\", \"A day on the moon, from one sunrise to the next, lasts about twenty-nine and a half Earth days, so the sun stays up for about two weeks at a time and then stays down for two weeks. With no air to hold the heat or spread it around, the ground changes more than anywhere on Earth. In sunlight it grows hotter than boiling water, and in the long night it becomes colder than anywhere on Earth, even the South Pole in winter. A machine left out through the night needs a heater, or its parts can crack in the cold. The sky stays black even at noon, because there is no air to scatter the sunlight and turn it blue. The first astronauts landed in the early morning of a moon day, when the ground was neither too hot nor too cold, and left long before the noon heat arrived.\", \"On the moon you weigh about one sixth of what you weigh on Earth, because the moon is much smaller and pulls on you less. An astronaut who can jump half a metre on Earth could jump about three metres there. But a space suit is stiff and heavy, and the knees and hips hardly bend inside it, so the astronauts found that big jumps made them fall over. Instead they moved in a kind of hop, pushing off with both feet like a kangaroo, and film of them shows slow, floating bounces across the ground. Falling was not dangerous in itself, because they came down slowly, but a sharp rock could tear a suit, so they tried to stay on their feet. Things they threw went further too: a golf ball hit on the moon in 1971 flew far beyond where the same swing would send it on Earth.\", \"The moon has no air, so there is no wind to blow its dust about, and no rain to wash it away. The footprints left by the first astronauts in 1969 are still there today, as sharp as the day they were made. Moon dust is not like the dust in a house. It is made of tiny broken pieces of rock and glass with sharp edges, because nothing has ever worn them smooth. It clung to the astronauts' suits and got into the joints, and brushing it off only pushed it deeper into the cloth. When they took their helmets off inside the lander, it smelled like burnt gunpowder. Unless a rock from space lands on them, the footprints could last for millions of years.\")}" width=34 at=canvas(1, 1)
    row bottom below=p gap=1 space=1 align=centre {
      text ask "Mia's summary: \"{pick(k, \"The moon spins once per trip round the Earth, so one side always faces us, and the far side gets as much sun as ours.\", \"Moon days and nights last two weeks each, burning hot and then freezing cold, under a black sky.\", \"You weigh a sixth as much on the moon, so jumps could go high and throws went much further.\", \"With no wind or rain, the 1969 footprints are still sharp and could last for millions of years.\")}\" She left out one main point. Which line does it start on?" width=29
      number-input answer
    }
  }

  answer pick(k, 4, 10, 4, 4)
  feedback {
    when (k != 1 and answer == 5) point=p {
      say "The point runs onto that line, but where does its first sentence begin?"
    }
    when (k == 1 and answer == 11) point=p {
      say "That line is part of the point, but its sentence begins earlier. Find the first word of it."
    }
    when (k == 3 and answer == 1) point=p {
      say "Mia kept the wind and the rain. Which idea about the moon has no tick in her summary?"
    }
  }
  hint "Tick each idea in Mia's summary off against the text."
  hint "Which main idea has no tick? Find where its sentence starts."
}
